    <statement><p>The figure below shows <m>f(x)</m> and its local linearization at
<m>x=a</m>, <m>y = 2 x - 4</m>.  (The local linearization is shown in
blue.)</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap3Sec9__Q11_pg-image-1.png" width="33%"/>
<p>What is the value of <m>a</m>?</p>
<p><m>a =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>What is the value of <m>f(a)</m>?</p>
<p><m>f(a) =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>Use the linearization to approximate the value of <m>f(4.4)</m>.</p>
<p><m>f(4.4) =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p>Is the approximation an under- or overestimate?</p>
<p><fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/> (Enter <alert>under</alert> or <alert>over</alert>.)</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4.8</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{under}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x=4</m>, we have <m>a=4</m>.  Since the
point with <m>x=4</m> lies on both the line and the curve, we have
<me>f(a) = f(4) = 2(4) - 4 = 4.</me>
The approximation is an underestimate because the line lies under the
curve.  Since the linear function approximates <m>f(x)</m>, we have
<me>f(4.4) \approx 2(4.4) - 4 = 4.8.</me>



</solution>
